// There is ONE range form in Burxt and it is exclusive. `..=` is refused in the LEXER, where
// it is read, rather than left to fall apart in the parser as `..` followed by `=`.
//
// Two forms differing by a single character, where that character changes the number of
// iterations, is exactly the defect a reviewer's eye slides over — and being reviewable is
// what this language is for. The cost is named: a loop that must touch `n` writes `0..n + 1`,
// which is a visible `+ 1` instead of an invisible `=`.
for i in 0..=3 {
    print(i);
}
